WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.940
Trying to find the right actors for a film
can be pretty difficult.

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:08.230
From the lead role, to an extra in the background,
every decision is pivotal to a film's success.

00:00:08.230 --> 00:00:12.759
In the following cases, the casting decisions
were so terrible that it left a lasting impression

00:00:12.759 --> 00:00:15.440
on us all, and not the one they were hoping
for.

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:19.090
Unfortunately for these actors, they were
caught in the crossfire for playing characters

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:20.670
that they just weren't right for.

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:22.710
Jesse Eisenberg as Lex Luthor

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:26.390
One of the all-time great comic book villains,
Lex Luthor is right up there with Magneto

00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:27.750
and Dr. Doom.

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:31.830
So when news broke that Jesse Eisenberg would
play Superman's arch-enemy in Zack Snyder's

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:36.940
Batman versus Superman: Dawn of Justice, let's
just say that fans weren't exactly pleased...

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:39.620
Things only got worse once people actually
saw the film.

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:44.420
While DC fans and movie critics usually don’t
see eye to eye, almost everyone agreed that

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:47.940
Eisenberg was totally miscast as the Man of
Steel's main villain.

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:52.109
Many believed that his interpretation of the
character was far too manic, way too loud,

00:00:52.109 --> 00:00:55.190
and too similar to a certain Clown Prince
of Crime.

00:00:55.190 --> 00:00:58.220
"Little red notes, big bang, you let your
family die!"

00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:02.310
While there's no denying Eisenberg is a brilliant
actor, here's hoping his Lex Luthor stays

00:01:02.310 --> 00:01:04.380
locked up for a long time.

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:12.590
“Ding ding ding ding ding”
“Ding ding ding ding ding”

00:01:12.590 --> 00:01:14.480
Scarlett Johansson as Major

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:19.500
One of the biggest casting scandals in recent
history started in 2015, when the world learned

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:24.360
that Scarlett Johansson was cast as Major
in a live-action remake of the 1995 film,

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:25.440
Ghost in the Shell.

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:29.490
The original is generally regarded as one
of the greatest animes of all time.

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:30.800
One problem?

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:31.800
Major is Japanese.

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:33.740
Johansson is not.

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:38.630
The news of her casting sparked fury, not
only by people who saw this as racially insensitive,

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:40.930
but by people who wanted an authentic film.

00:01:40.930 --> 00:01:44.200
Over 100,000 people signed a petition in protest.

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:49.040
Advocacy Groups condemned the news, and soon
#WhiteWashedOut was trending on Twitter.

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:52.960
The film ended up bombing at the box office
and some suspect that the critical backlash

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:55.820
surrounding the whitewashing incident was
the reason for it.

00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:59.460
Hopefully, the Scarlett Johansson controversy
will convince Hollywood to be a little more

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:03.610
diverse when it comes to casting—although
it'll probably just inspire studios to stop

00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:06.460
remaking anime movies altogether.

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:08.640
Mark Wahlberg as Elliot Moore

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:12.710
By and large, M. Night Shyamalan has a pretty
solid track record when it comes to casting.

00:02:12.710 --> 00:02:17.650
But as far as casting mistakes go, it doesn't
get much worse than Mark Wahlberg in The Happening.

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:19.950
Put Wahlberg in the right film, you get movie
gold.

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:23.540
But shove him into the wrong movie and you'll
send your audience into the wrong kind of

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:24.540
hysterics.

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:28.570
And in this eco-thriller, Wahlberg is definitely
in the wrong role, playing a panicked high

00:02:28.570 --> 00:02:32.640
school science teacher with some of the worst
line delivery in cinematic history.

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:35.600
"Plan on murdering me in my sleep."

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:36.800
"What? No!"

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:41.120
In all fairness, Shyamalan's script isn't
doing Wahlberg any favors, but the actor does

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:44.010
bring an extra special awfulness to the film.

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:48.290
Even Wahlberg himself admitted the film wasn't
his best work, saying that it was "a bad movie."

00:02:48.290 --> 00:02:51.950
Still, we kind of sympathize with the guy;
he probably just wanted to try something different.

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:54.400
“Just a second”
"There were children in that room."

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:56.560
"Elliot please tell us what to do."

00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:57.060
"I need a second.

00:02:57.260 --> 00:02:59.540
Why can't anyone give me a goddamn second?"

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:01.520
Robert De Niro as The Creature

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:06.100
The legendary Robert De Niro's experienced
a rare flop with his performance as "The Creature"

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:07.540
in Mary Shelley's Frankenstein.

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:11.800
Sure, he might've been less ridiculous if
he had less ridiculous makeup, but the problems

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:12.960
didn't end there.

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:18.090
His portrayal was also panned for maintaining
a modern New York accent in mid-1700s Geneva.

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:22.090
The fact that he was still the same "You talkin'
to me" De Niro that we all know and love was

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:25.900
distracting at best and absolutely terrible
at worst.

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:27.930
Kevin Spacey as Bobby Darin

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:32.850
When singer Bobby Darin died at 37, his legacy
left a big impact on the music world.

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:36.970
Among his dedicated fans was Kevin Spacey,
who spent more than a decade trying to make

00:03:36.970 --> 00:03:39.890
Beyond The Sea, a bio-pic about Darin's life.

00:03:39.890 --> 00:03:44.010
Most studios were reluctant to finance the
film because Spacey wanted to produce, direct

00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:47.880
and play the lead… even though he was significantly
older than Darin.

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:51.110
Spacey stuck to his guns and eventually got
the deal done with Lionsgate.

00:03:51.110 --> 00:03:54.640
Many thought that he did a great job performing
Darin's songs, but that there was something

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:58.960
weird about watching a man in his mid-40s
playing a character in his late teens.

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:03.560
"You don’t care about people hun, you
don't give a damn about 'em."

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:12.800
New York Magazine wrote that Spacey was "too
jowly" for the role, adding that it felt "downright

00:04:12.810 --> 00:04:15.349
pervy" to watch his love scenes with Kate
Bosworth.

00:04:15.349 --> 00:04:19.040
So while you've got to give Spacey points
for dedication, casting someone who wasn't

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:22.139
two decades too old for the part would've
been a better move.

00:04:22.139 --> 00:04:24.240
Jennifer Lawrence as Joy Mangano

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:28.270
Everybody knows Hollywood has a major problem
when it comes to casting women over 30.

00:04:28.270 --> 00:04:31.900
That means a lot of younger actresses get
parts that are probably better suited for

00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:34.230
ladies with a bit more life experience.

00:04:34.230 --> 00:04:37.750
If you need proof, look no further than David
O. Russell’s casting of Jennifer Lawrence

00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:38.750
in Joy.

00:04:38.750 --> 00:04:44.060
This fact-based 2015 film focused on Joy Mangano,
a 34-year-old single mom who overcame life's

00:04:44.060 --> 00:04:48.980
obstacles by creating a self-wringing mop
and finding success as a TV personality.

00:04:48.980 --> 00:04:52.720
As the movie centers on a woman in her mid-30s,
it might've worked better if the role had

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:54.950
gone to someone in that same age bracket.

00:04:54.950 --> 00:04:58.410
Even Lawrence confessed she was too young
for the role, but she justified it by saying

00:04:58.410 --> 00:05:01.670
Russell doesn't worry about "silly questions"
like age.

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:05.750
That sounds nice when you're getting high-profile
parts, but "older" actresses might not think

00:05:05.750 --> 00:05:08.160
those questions are quite so silly.

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:11.240
Sofia Coppola in the The Godfather: Part III

00:05:11.240 --> 00:05:15.060
When Francis Ford Coppola made the decision
to put his daughter Sofia in the role of Mary

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:18.790
Corleone in the third Godfather film, the
results were disastrous.

00:05:18.790 --> 00:05:23.350
Whereas the first two films in the Godfather
trilogy were exceptionally good, Part III

00:05:23.350 --> 00:05:24.350
was a mess.

00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:28.100
Her stiffness on-screen was palpable, especially
in the moments when she shared the screen

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:29.480
with her more established co-stars.

00:05:36.860 --> 00:05:37.360
"Dad?"

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:42.780
Mickey Rooney as Mr. Yunioshi

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:44.810
File this one under "never again".

00:05:44.810 --> 00:05:49.280
Mickey Rooney's cartoonish portrayal of Mr.
Yunioshi in Breakfast at Tiffany's is basically

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:50.400
unwatchable.

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:53.980
Rooney was widely respected, thanks to the
rest of his immense body of work, but the

00:05:53.980 --> 00:05:58.620
image of him wandering around with taped eyelids
and large false teeth is hard to unsee.

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:03.360
So much so that the film, which is otherwise
revered for Audrey Hepburn's iconic lead performance,

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:06.960
has been consistently hit with backlash for
its cultural stereotyping.

00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:15.220
Thanks for watching!

